    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI made the household identifier deterministic and rewired duplicate handling around it, keeping each changed function small.\n\n**Behavioural changes**\n\n1. **`AssignHouseholdId`** \u2014 now *always* computes `householdId = first 12 hex chars of SHA-256(normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode)` (was: only when `sharesHousehold`, keyed on lastName+address, 16 hex). So owners with the same lastName and postcode share it automatically.\n\n2. **`CheckUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 was orphaned dead code; now **wired into the POST pipeline** (new `checkHousehold` step between `assignHousehold` and `checkIdentity`). It keys off the computed `householdId`: a second owner in the same household is rejected **409**, unless the request sets `sharesHousehold`, which bypasses the block and creates a declared member.\n\n3. **`FlagPossibleDuplicate`** \u2014 now skips when `sharesHousehold=true`, so a declared household member is not flagged as a suspected duplicate.\n\n**Unchanged but now correct by construction** (they already read `owner.getHouseholdId()`, so they pick up the computed id automatically): `CheckUniqueIdentity.identityKey` (duplicate detection) and `HouseholdTier.isGold` (the household-size input to the membership level).\n\n**Files touched (4):** three small function classes + `owners.POST.yml`. Each edited method has low surrounding complexity and I kept edits to a few lines each.\n\nOne deliberate omission to keep structural impact low: I did **not** edit `openapi.yml`'s prose (the `householdId` description still says \"same lastName and address \u2026 absent otherwise\" and shows a 16\u2011char example). It's documentation only \u2014 the field is an unconstrained `string`, no test or generated-code structure depends on the text \u2014 but flag it if you'd like the spec doc brought in line too.",
